// t-tvar-03: a `<-` target bound to a lambda is generalized; the writer
// unifies into the scheme, so it narrows only the uses checked after it.
//
// `let f = |x| x` puts f in poly_binds (graphix-compiler/src/node/bind.rs:232).
// `f(41)` instantiates the scheme fn('a) -> 'a at i64. The connect's check
// (node/mod.rs:2334, bind.typ.check_contains) then binds the scheme's own
// generic 'a to string, after that use was typed. With lines 2 and 3 swapped
// the program is refused ("string does not contain i64").
//
// command: graphix --check design/review-2026-10-05/repro/t-tvar-03.gx
//          graphix --no-cache design/review-2026-10-05/repro/t-tvar-03.gx
//          graphix --no-cache --no-fusion design/review-2026-10-05/repro/t-tvar-03.gx
//
// expected: --check refuses it in either statement order (the writer does
//   not fit the fn('a) -> 'a that f(41) used, or f, being written, is not
//   generalized and f(41) fixes 'a = i64).
// observed: --check exits 0. The default run panics the runtime thread:
//   "panicked at graphix-compiler/src/fusion/kernel.rs:243:13: kernel param
//   `n`: runtime String("oops") does not match the compiled Scalar(I64)
//   slot", then "Error: runtime did not respond", exit 1. --no-fusion prints
//   42, then logs "arith error ... Parse error ... Unexpected `o`". With
//   RUST_LOG=error --log-dir, both binds log "a run-time bind at f(41) did
//   not type: an instance at fn(x: i64) -> i64 of a definition typed ..."
//   (the identity's own definition was narrowed by the writer too).
//
// The same hole, two other ways (both pass --check, both log an arith error):
//   let id = |x| x; let y = never(); y <- id; let r = y("s"); y <- |x: i64| x + 1; r
//   let f = |x| x; let fr = &f; *fr <- |x: i64| x + 1; let g = f; g("s")
let f = |x| x;
let n = f(41);
f <- |x| "oops";
n + 1
